// policyField describes one content field of Policy: how to read it and
// how to write it back. policyFieldList is the single definition of which
// fields a policy has and in what order the fingerprint encoder, the
// archive codec and the invalid-UTF-8 scan visit them.
type policyField struct {
    name string
    // str is non-nil for string fields; boolean fields use boolean instead.
    str     func(p *Policy) string
    setStr  func(p *Policy, v string)
    boolean func(p *Policy) bool
    setBool func(p *Policy, v bool)
    // omitEmptyInFingerprint reports that the fingerprint skips this
    // string field when empty, mirroring the JSON envelope's omitempty.
    omitEmptyInFingerprint bool
}

var policyFieldList = []policyField{
    {name: "ID", str: func(p *Policy) string { return p.ID }, setStr: func(p *Policy, v string) { p.ID = v }},
    {name: "Subject", ...},
    {name: "Action", ...},
    {name: "Scope", ...},
    {name: "Effect", str: func(p *Policy) string { return string(p.Effect) }, setStr: func(p *Policy, v string) { p.Effect = Effect(v) }},
    {name: "Recursive", boolean: ..., setBool: ...},
    {name: "ResourceID", ..., omitEmptyInFingerprint: true},
}
```

Then:

UTF-8 scan:
```go
func policyUsesRawBytes(p *Policy) bool {
    for _, f := range policyFieldList {
        if f.str != nil && !utf8.ValidString(f.str(p)) {
            return true
        }
    }
    return false
}
```

Fingerprint encoder:
```go
func (e *fingerprintEncoder) policy(p Policy) {
    e.tag(tagPolicy)
    for _, f := range policyFieldList {
        if f.str != nil {
            v := f.str(&p)
            if f.omitEmptyInFingerprint && v == "" {
                continue
            }
            e.rawString(v)
        } else {
            e.boolTag(tagBool, f.boolean(&p))
        }
    }
}
```

Wait — but the current fingerprint code has the ResourceID skip *after* writing the bool. With the table, order is ID..Effect, Recursive(bool), ResourceID — matches. Good.

Archive writer:
```go
func (w *archiveWriter) policy(p Policy) {
    for _, f := range policyFieldList {
        if f.str != nil {
            w.stringField(f.str(&p))
        } else {
            w.boolean(f.boolean(&p))
        }
    }
}
```

Archive reader:
```go
func (r *archiveReader) policy() Policy {
    var p Policy
    for _, f := range policyFieldList {
        if f.setStr != nil {
            f.setStr(&p, r.stringField())
        } else {
            f.setBool(&p, r.boolean())
        }
    }
    return p
}
```

Where should this table live? The Policy type is in core.go presumably. The table is used by audit.go (fingerprint, utf8 scan) and audit_archive.go. Put it in core.go near Policy, or in audit.go. Let me check core.go for Policy definition.

Also check whether there are other places listing policy fields: maybe org.go (authorization rules — matching logic), recheck.go, offline_review.go. The task says "保留现有策略字段和授权规则" — keep existing fields and authorization rules; the refactor targets archive read/write, fingerprint, and UTF-8 check. But maybe policy matching also enumerates fields? Not necessarily — matching uses fields individually, that's fine.

Also the JSON envelope: PolicyChange serialized via encoding/json uses Policy's JSON tags in core.go. The `omitempty` on ResourceID must stay. The table's omitEmptyInFingerprint mirrors it; add comment.

